{"kind":"report","text":"Ready for review at exact clean head 6a20f2219a4ad56ee0eac9fa2b60b8ff1fd7c83c on task/ui-interpretation-in-context. This replaces 203a8ab8. Both of codex's findings were correct and I verified each against the code before changing anything.\n\nF1, and it was the sharper of the two. My consequence text overclaimed in both branches of a feature whose entire purpose was to stop the surface saying untrue things about acts.\n\nFor undefined-kind I wrote that a citation never formed. fold.go projects rests_on provenance for every record before it judges any of them, so an unreadable act still cites what it cited. I checked the two live undefined-kind acts, 4f699949 and 518aadc9, and each carries a projected citation edge right now. The sentence was false about the very acts that prompted this work.\n\nFor uninterpretable I wrote 'until the interpreter is bound', which promises that binding fixes it. The same verdict also covers kind definitions that are permanently invalid and prohibited redefinitions, which no later interpreter can rescue.\n\nThe notice now keys on the reason rather than the verdict, because only the unbound-interpreter case names itself remediable. Undefined kind says no rule in this room reads a kind by this name, so the act is recorded without force and nothing here acts on it - true without enumerating edges that do form. The two uninterpretable classes are separated: one says unless one is bound, the other says the reason is not something a later interpreter would resolve. Tests pin all three and forbid the citation claim by assertion, so neither overclaim can return.\n\nF2. The head was four commits behind main and genuinely conflicted, in ui/test/components.test.mjs where the thread railway landed and in the generated bundle. Resolved keeping both sides: the railway's three rendered tests and the unreadable-act regression, five tests in that file. The bundle is regenerated from the combined sources rather than taken from either side, and jsdom arrives with main's dom test.\n\nI made a mistake resolving it that is worth recording. My first pass stripped conflict markers mechanically, which silently removed my own test's closing block and left a file that parsed as truncated. Node reported only 'Unexpected end of input'. I repaired it by inspection rather than repeating the strip. A mechanical resolution of a conflict that spans a function boundary can produce a file that is syntactically wrong in a way the diff does not show.\n\nVerified at this head: current main is an ancestor; tsc clean; npm test 50 of 50, the count codex measured for a correct resolution; npm run build with the committed bundle byte-identical when rebuilt from scratch in a separate worktree; make vet and make test pass. The three mutations from the previous round still turn the rendered regression red, and the new class distinctions are pinned by assertion.\n\nUnchanged from the previous report and still true: the document.title render guard is the minimal precondition for rendering any act in test, and the obsolete gap-grouping test was replaced rather than kept because the behaviour it pinned no longer exists.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:49656651aad034603e2d964c224227205e102591","branch":"task/ui-interpretation-in-context","head":"6a20f2219a4ad56ee0eac9fa2b60b8ff1fd7c83c"}}